ShareAlike is deliberate rather than incidental. This dataset was assembled from
sources that themselves share alike — OpenStreetMap, Wikimedia Commons — and the
same terms are what let its photographs sit alongside its prose without conflict.

Note that the dataset's structure is expressed in `provenance` on every record:
a confidence level, the sources behind the claim, and a `needs_verification`
ledger. That structure is part of what is licensed. If you republish records,
republish the provenance with them; a claim from this dataset stripped of its
confidence level is a claim this project did not make.


2. Geometry derived from OpenStreetMap
--------------------------------------

Covers: `web/lib/geo.json` (district boundaries and river courses, from the
Overpass API), `web/lib/routes.json` (road route geometry, from OSRM), and any
coordinate in `data/` traced from OSM rather than observed directly.

Licensed under the Open Database License 1.0 (ODbL).

    https://opendatacommons.org/licenses/odbl/1-0/

    © OpenStreetMap contributors

ODbL's share-alike attaches to derived databases, so this carve-out survives
reuse: extract the boundaries or the route geometry into your own product and
the ODbL travels with them, independently of the CC BY-SA covering the rest.
Both files carry a `_license` field stating this at their head — keep it.

Routing geometry was produced by OSRM (`router.project-osrm.org`) operating over
OSM data, so it is an OSM derivative even though no OSM file was downloaded.


3. Photographs and video
------------------------

Covers: `web/public/images/`, `web/public/videos/`.

**Licensed individually, per file.** There is no blanket licence for this
directory and none may be assumed. Each image is declared in its spot record
under `media.images`, and each entry carries the licence, the credit and a
`source_url` back to the original:

    {
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/...",
      "license": "cc-by-sa",
      "caption": "Hatgadh village and hills seen from the fort",
      "credit": "Dinesh Valke",
      "source_url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:..."
    }

The licence values in use are:

| Value      | Meaning                                                          |
|------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|
| `own`      | Photographed by Rushi Chudasama; released under CC BY-SA 4.0     |
| `cc-by-sa` | Wikimedia Commons contributor, CC BY-SA — credit and share alike |
| `cc-by`    | Wikimedia Commons contributor, CC BY — credit required           |
| `cc0`      | Public domain dedication, no conditions                          |

All video under `web/public/videos/` is the maintainer's own footage, declared
in `media.videos` with `source: "Rushi Chudasama (own footage)"`, and is released
under CC BY-SA 4.0 on the same terms as the dataset.

**If you reuse a photograph, carry its credit.** The `credit` field names a real
person who licensed their work on the condition that they are named. Dropping it
is a licence violation against that photographer, not against this project.

Entries pair with staged files strictly by index — `media.images[0]` describes
`<spot-id>.jpg`, `media.images[1]` describes `<spot-id>-2.jpg`, and so on. The
`url` field records where a file came from and is never used for matching. A
surplus entry therefore hands its credit to the next photograph added, which is
an attribution hazard rather than an untidiness; `scripts/validate.mjs` fails the
build on a count mismatch for exactly this reason.


4. What is not licensed here
----------------------------

Every record cites its sources in `provenance.sources` — government portals,
travel publishers, blogs, Google Maps, YouTube. Those citations establish where
a fact was checked. They do not transfer any rights in the cited work, and no
text from them is reproduced. Their content remains theirs.


5. No warranty
--------------

This material is provided as-is. Fees, timings, road conditions and booking
arrangements in this belt change without notice, and several records are stamped
with a confidence level below `high` precisely because they could not be
independently confirmed. Verify volatile details before you travel or before you
depend on them in a product. Nothing here is a guarantee of safety, access or
accuracy.
